em, I can keep one as the pair. For example, if I discard T6, then B7 remains as the pair. If I discard B7, T6 remains. Which one is better? Well, both are possible. But which is safer to discard?\n\nIf I discard T6, there are 2 left in the wall. If someone has T5 and T7, they might need T6. If I discard B7, someone with B6 and B8 might need B7. Since I have two B7s, maybe it's better to discard one to reduce the chance of others completing a chow.\n\nBut I need to decide between discarding T6 or B7. Alternatively, discard an honor tile.\n\nAlternatively, maybe discard a tile that's not part of any pair or chow. For example, W1. But W1 is a terminal, and discarding it might not affect the All Simples (since the pair is non-terminal). However, discarding W1 leaves 3 in the wall, so it's safe.\n\nBut since I have All Simples, which requires that all tiles except the pair are simples. The pair is T6 or B7, which are simples. So discarding W1 (a terminal) is okay for All Simples. But All Simples is 2 points, which is part of the total. So it's safe.\n\nBut maybe it's better to discard a tile that's not part of the All Simples pattern. Wait, All Simples requires that all tiles except the pair are simples. The pair is part of the hand, so if the pair is a terminal or honor, it's not part of the All Simples. But in this case, the pair is T6 or B7, which are simples. So All Simples is satisfied.\n\nSo discarding W1 (a terminal) is safe. But maybe it's better to discard a tile that's not part of any possible chow or pair. For example, F1.\n\nConsidering all options, I think the best move is to discard a tile that is not part of any possible chow or pair and has a high number of remaining tiles. For example, F1 or F4. Since they are honors and have 4 left, discarding them is safe and doesn't hinder my patterns.\n\nBetween F1 and F4, I have F1 (East) and F4 (North). The prevailing wind is West, and my seat wind is East. Prevalent Wind is West, so if I have a pung of West, it's