aks the 6-7-8 Chi. \n\nThe best option is to keep the 6-7-8 Chi intact. So discarding any of W6, W7, W8 would break that potential. Discarding W2 might not help. \n\nIf the player discards T6, they lose a pair but can still have B7 as a pair. Let's see:\n\nIf they discard T6, the hand has B7 (2) as a pair. The melds are [B1-B2-B3] and [W6-W7-W8] (if they have all three). So the hand would have two melds and a pair. Need two more melds. \n\nWait, the hand after discarding T6 would be [W1:2, W2:1, W6:1, W7:1, W8:1, B2:1, B7:2]. The melds are [B1-B2-B3] and [W6-W7-W8]. So two melds and a pair of B7. Still need two more melds. \n\nIf the player can form a Pung of W1 (if they have two and get another), but they have two W1s. If they can get a third, they can form a Pung. \n\nAlternatively, if they have W1 (2), W2 (1), and can form a W1-W2-W3 Chi if they get a W3. \n\nBut this is speculative. \n\nThe best move might be to discard T6, keeping the 6-7-8 Chi and the pair of B7. Alternatively, discard W2 to keep the possibility of forming a W1-W2-W3 Chi if W3 is available. \n\nBut since the player has W6, W7, W8, forming a 6-7-8 Chi is more immediate. So keeping those intact is better. \n\nTherefore, the best action is to discard T6, which is a pair but allows the 6-7-8 Chi to be formed. \n\nWait, but the player has T6 as a pair. If they discard T6, they lose the pair. However, they can still use B7 as a pair. \n\nSo the answer is to Play T6.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The hand has a pair of T6 and potential for forming a 6-7-8 Chow in the W suit. Discarding T6 preserves the 6-7-8 Chow and allows the B7 pair to serve as the final pair for the winning hand.\nReason: Discarding T6 maintains the critical 6-7-8 Chow in W suit and keeps the B7 pair intact, maximizing the chance to complete the hand with two melds (Chi and 6-7-8 Chow) and the pair. Other options risk breaking the 6-7-8 sequence or losing key pairs.\nAnswer: Play T6"}
