lpful. W8 is part of W6-W7-W8, but again, in a meld. B7 is part of B6-B7-B8. So maybe playing B7 is better, as it's part of a potential chow. But the user has B8, so if they play B7, they can still have B8. If they play B8, they lose the pair. Hmm.\\n\\nAlternatively, if the user plays B7, then they have W8, T8, B8, B8. The B8s are a pair. The W8 and T8 can't form a chow. But maybe the fourth meld is a pung of B8s if they draw another. Since there are two B8s left, that's possible. If they draw a B8, they can form a pung, which would add 1 point (Pung of Terminals or Honors, but B8 isn't a terminal). Wait, Pung of Terminals or Honors is for pungs of terminals (1 or 9) or honors. B8 is a middle tile. So that pattern wouldn't apply. But having a pung of B8s would count as a Pung (which is part of All Pungs if they have four pungs, but here it's just one). So maybe not. But the pung itself is part of the hand structure.\\n\\nAlternatively, if the user plays B8, then they have B7 and B8, which could form a chow with B6. But again, need B6. So maybe the best is to play a tile that's not part of any potential chow for others. Let's look at the unshown tiles. For example, W8 has 2 left. If the user plays W8, others might need it for a chow. T8 has 2 left. B7 has 1 left. B8 has 2 left. So playing B7 might be the least useful for others. Because B7 is only 1 left. If the user plays B7, there's only one left, which might be less useful for others. Whereas B8 has 2 left, which others might need. So maybe playing B7 is better.\\n\\nBut the user's hand would then be W8, T8, B8, B8. The B8s are a pair. The other tiles are single. To form a fourth meld, they need a chow or pung. The B8s could be a pung if they draw another B8. The W8 and T8 can't form a chow. So the fourth meld would have to be a pung of B8s. If they can get another B8, that's possible. The unshown B8s are 2. So the chance is there. So playing B7 might be better to keep the pair of B8s and hope to draw another B8.\\n\\nAlternatively, if the user plays B8, they have B7 and one B8. Then the pair is gone, and they need to form another pair. But the other tiles are single. So that's worse.\\n\\nSo the best move is to play B7, keeping the pair of B8s and the possibility to form a pung if they draw another B8. Also, B7 is less likely to be needed by others since only one left. So the answer is Play B7.\\n\", \"output\": \"Evaluation: Playing B7 allows retaining a pair of B8s, which could form a pung if another B8 is drawn, contributing to potential scoring patterns. B7 is less likely to be needed by others as only one remains.\\nReason: Retaining B8s for a possible pung and reducing discard utility for opponents.\\nAnswer: Play B7\"}"}
