r of B8s. If they draw a B8, they can form a pung. So in this case, playing T8 keeps the pair of B8s. So that might be better.\n\nWait, the user's goal is to maximize their chances of completing the hand. If they play T8, they keep the pair of B8s. If they draw a B8, they can form a pung. If they play B8, they have one B8 left. If they draw a B8, they can form a pair again. But to form a pung, they need two more B8s. So playing T8 is better because it keeps the pair of B8s, making it easier to form a pung with one more B8.\n\nTherefore, the best move is to play T8, keeping the pair of B8s. This way, if they draw a B8, they can form a pung and complete the hand. Playing T8 also removes a tile that might be needed by others, but that's a secondary consideration.\n\nSo the answer should be to play T8.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Playing T8 allows retaining a pair of B8s, which can be upgraded to a pung if another B8 is drawn. This maximizes the chance to complete the hand efficiently.  \nReason: The hand requires a fourth meld. Keeping two B8s provides a direct path to a pung of B8s (needs one more B8). Discarding T8 avoids wasting a terminal tile and maintains flexibility for other scoring patterns.  \nAnswer: Play T8"}
